1
00:00:01,297 --> 00:00:06,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is the Chanda Sutta, Samyutta Nikaya, 42.1.

2
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:28,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once at Sarvati, Chanda approached the Blessed One, paid homage to Him, sat down to One's side and said, Venerable Sir, what is the cause and reason why someone here is known as rough, and what is the cause and reason why someone here is known as gentle?

3
00:00:29,207 --> 00:00:29,427
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

4
00:00:31,813 --> 00:00:41,797
[SPEAKER_00]: I think he's asking if, in this, in your dhamma and discipline, who is rough and who is gentle, according to the Buddha's understanding.

5
00:00:42,838 --> 00:00:48,020
[SPEAKER_00]: And so the Buddha replies, here, someone has not abandoned lust.

6
00:00:48,820 --> 00:00:53,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he has not abandoned lust, others irritate him.

7
00:00:53,142 --> 00:00:56,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Being irritated by others, he manifests his irritation.

8
00:00:57,163 --> 00:00:58,324
[SPEAKER_00]: He is known as rough.

9
00:01:00,868 --> 00:01:05,132
[SPEAKER_00]: He has not abandoned hatred, because he has not abandoned hatred, others irritate him.

10
00:01:05,372 --> 00:01:08,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Being irritated by others, he manifests irritation.

11
00:01:08,695 --> 00:01:09,496
[SPEAKER_00]: He is known as rough.

12
00:01:10,377 --> 00:01:18,264
[SPEAKER_00]: He has not abandoned distraction, or avijja, delusion, because he has not abandoned distraction, others irritate him.

13
00:01:18,684 --> 00:01:21,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Being irritated by others, he manifests irritation.

14
00:01:21,266 --> 00:01:22,147
[SPEAKER_00]: He is known as rough.

15
00:01:22,828 --> 00:01:26,391
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the cause and reason why one can be known as rough.

16
00:01:30,119 --> 00:01:32,480
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, here someone has abandoned lust.

17
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:35,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he has abandoned lust, others do not irritate him.

18
00:01:36,481 --> 00:01:39,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Not being irritated by others, he does not manifest irritation.

19
00:01:40,282 --> 00:01:41,263
[SPEAKER_00]: He is known as gentle.

20
00:01:42,043 --> 00:01:44,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Likewise with hatred and distraction, delusion.

21
00:01:46,124 --> 00:01:49,845
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the cause and the reason why one can be known as gentle.

22
00:01:51,826 --> 00:01:56,368
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I like this sutta because it...

23
00:01:56,428 --> 00:01:57,428
[SPEAKER_00]: I know from experience,

24
00:01:58,836 --> 00:02:06,521
[SPEAKER_00]: What is known as rough and gentle in the world is outward expression, outward forms.

25
00:02:06,541 --> 00:02:08,703
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is another sutra which says that, doesn't it?

26
00:02:09,363 --> 00:02:11,865
[SPEAKER_00]: About the one monk who was...

27
00:02:11,885 --> 00:02:20,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Companions in the holy life, like softly spoken, always pleasing, nice to be around, but of the wrong view, and you should avoid them completely.

28
00:02:21,732 --> 00:02:22,913
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't have anything to do with them.

29
00:02:23,994 --> 00:02:25,775
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the alternative was like you have

30
00:02:26,907 --> 00:02:33,148
[SPEAKER_01]: Roughly spoken, not easy to be around, difficult, but of the right view.

31
00:02:33,168 --> 00:02:34,789
[SPEAKER_00]: And you should, you should go and be with that.

32
00:02:35,509 --> 00:02:37,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what really matters at the end of the day.

33
00:02:40,170 --> 00:02:41,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, of course, you can have both.

34
00:02:43,211 --> 00:02:43,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Or neither.

35
00:02:44,271 --> 00:02:46,152
[SPEAKER_01]: You could have softly spoken and the right view.

36
00:02:47,532 --> 00:02:52,073
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is that the emphasis should be on whether it's the right view or not.

37
00:02:53,913 --> 00:02:56,534
[SPEAKER_01]: That, that kind of underlines the behavior of this person.

38
00:02:57,277 --> 00:03:00,299
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, you can't just use the external appearance as a criteria.

39
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:14,790
[SPEAKER_00]: So... Yeah, there's this... Yeah, so that's the issues that people want to use external things, like clothing, way of moving the body...

40
00:03:14,810 --> 00:03:15,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

41
00:03:15,351 --> 00:03:16,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Way of speaking...

42
00:03:16,151 --> 00:03:18,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Uniform, decorum...

43
00:03:19,108 --> 00:03:25,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, again, all fine if it's for like a, I don't know, for like a social harmony.

44
00:03:26,114 --> 00:03:30,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you don't go and just start telling off a random stranger, you know.

45
00:03:30,538 --> 00:03:35,162
[SPEAKER_01]: But if somebody comes to learn the dhamma from you, they're not a stranger.

46
00:03:35,182 --> 00:03:36,823
[SPEAKER_01]: It means they want to learn.

47
00:03:38,565 --> 00:03:40,506
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, if you come across, like,

48
00:03:41,595 --> 00:03:44,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Not so pleasing, in your expression.

49
00:03:44,838 --> 00:03:49,181
[SPEAKER_01]: And if they refuse then to hear the dhamma because of how you come across, well, that's their great loss.

50
00:03:50,902 --> 00:03:57,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like, you read the vinaya stories in the sutras, I mean, the Buddha was far from mildly mannered and softly spoken.

51
00:03:58,628 --> 00:04:06,134
[SPEAKER_01]: When monks did something wrong, like, the names he called them, the comparisons he'd drawn between them and what they did, it was quite horrific.

52
00:04:07,355 --> 00:04:10,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of the weight of their actions, it was appropriate.

53
00:04:11,745 --> 00:04:13,507
[SPEAKER_01]: He wasn't there to pat you on the back.

54
00:04:14,088 --> 00:04:16,711
[SPEAKER_01]: He was there to lead you out of saṃsāra, basically.

55
00:04:17,553 --> 00:04:22,519
[SPEAKER_01]: And you think that that can be done with just like some skillful communication and we all feel good?

56
00:04:22,639 --> 00:04:22,819
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

57
00:04:24,221 --> 00:04:27,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the world doesn't... can't be solved by that.

58
00:04:27,325 --> 00:04:28,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, that's not the root of the problem.

59
00:04:30,844 --> 00:04:42,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you... if your emphasis is on the level of how you communicate, how you come across, not why you do so, in other words, because of greed, aversion or delusion, you'll never solve the problem.

60
00:04:43,571 --> 00:04:55,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if everybody never utters any bad words, again, their intentions remain infected with lust, with aversion, delusion, which sooner or later conflict will have to arise, because that's the root of conflict.

61
00:04:57,559 --> 00:04:58,280
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the problem.

62
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:00,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, as I said, you can

63
00:05:01,779 --> 00:05:02,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever.

64
00:05:02,460 --> 00:05:07,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Learn the manners, and learn how to speak properly, and just... it will basically cause you less hassle in the world.

65
00:05:07,423 --> 00:05:13,788
[SPEAKER_01]: People won't... won't attack you, won't... won't be against you, necessarily, but... there is a limit to that.

66
00:05:14,909 --> 00:05:18,892
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem is, like, if you start confusing, eh...

67
00:05:20,978 --> 00:05:30,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Learning a communication skill and how to express without offending anybody and so on, you start confusing that, as the development in the dhamma, that you're completely misguided.

68
00:05:31,983 --> 00:05:33,203
[SPEAKER_01]: And people do confuse that.

69
00:05:33,743 --> 00:05:43,247
[SPEAKER_01]: People, more often than not, they confuse psychotherapy and... which a lot revolves around learning the non-violent communication and learning all of this.

70
00:05:43,608 --> 00:05:46,009
[SPEAKER_01]: They confuse that for the actual practice of the dhamma.

71
00:05:46,589 --> 00:05:47,909
[SPEAKER_01]: People, they've been exposed to both.

72
00:05:47,969 --> 00:05:49,030
[SPEAKER_01]: I've seen that so many times.

73
00:05:49,836 --> 00:06:14,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yes, you've actually become better in worldly terms, but no amount of you purifying your expression, cleaning your words up and perfecting your outward shell, no amount of that would bring you any closer to uprooting the greed, aversion, delusion.

74
00:06:15,847 --> 00:06:16,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Not an inch closer.

75
00:06:17,740 --> 00:06:24,684
[SPEAKER_01]: It will probably take you further from it, because, as I said, now you're confusing a random worldly practice for the practice of the dhamma.

76
00:06:25,184 --> 00:06:28,887
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, you won't look for what the actual practice of the dhamma is.

77
00:06:30,348 --> 00:06:34,250
[SPEAKER_00]: And so you can polish your outward shell all you want.

78
00:06:35,050 --> 00:06:37,693
[SPEAKER_00]: But, internally, it's absolutely filthy.

79
00:06:37,933 --> 00:06:38,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

80
00:06:38,213 --> 00:06:38,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Still.

81
00:06:38,573 --> 00:06:38,774
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

82
00:06:38,794 --> 00:06:39,955
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you speak yourself.

83
00:06:39,975 --> 00:06:40,695
[SPEAKER_01]: The asavas are still there.

84
00:06:40,715 --> 00:06:42,317
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you are still irritated.

85
00:06:42,637 --> 00:06:45,160
[SPEAKER_00]: And, if you are still irritated... That's the root of the problem.

86
00:06:45,180 --> 00:06:45,680
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the problem.

87
00:06:46,180 --> 00:06:55,109
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how, how softly, the words you're using, or how you're walking slowly, and you, you know, doing all the externally right things.

88
00:06:55,709 --> 00:06:57,411
[SPEAKER_00]: You're coming from a place of irritation.

89
00:06:58,074 --> 00:07:00,075
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha said that in that...

90
00:07:00,216 --> 00:07:04,559
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't remember the reference, but it was basically when he said it was a perfect harmony.

91
00:07:05,619 --> 00:07:12,264
[SPEAKER_01]: When the community is perfectly harmonious, not if they learn how to talk to each other, or if they never use rough words.

92
00:07:12,985 --> 00:07:16,467
[SPEAKER_01]: No, they're perfectly harmonious if they all partake in the same right view.

93
00:07:17,108 --> 00:07:19,049
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sotāpati is the result of harmony.

94
00:07:21,035 --> 00:07:34,319
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, consequently, like, like, you could, you could, you could then conclude very easily that, okay, so without the right view, even if the entire community is just all smiles and happiness and nice words, that's not harmony.

95
00:07:35,059 --> 00:07:45,863
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically circumstantial agreeability, that will have to go apart, break apart at some point, because the root for the

96
00:07:49,304 --> 00:07:51,206
[SPEAKER_01]: That division still exists.

97
00:07:51,726 --> 00:07:54,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Greed averse delusion, sensuality, desires.

98
00:07:55,649 --> 00:07:56,670
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the root of suffering.

99
00:07:57,990 --> 00:08:05,616
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're not free from desires, no amount of you padding your expression and how you come across and all of that will change that.

100
00:08:07,197 --> 00:08:13,622
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, it will factually make your life easier and it's fine to do it for that reason.

101
00:08:14,607 --> 00:08:25,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, if you keep going around and whatever you say, people just constantly upset with you and harassing you all the time as a result of it, just on a worldly basis, because of how you speak, that harassment is a nuisance.

102
00:08:25,970 --> 00:08:30,050
[SPEAKER_01]: So you just change the way you speak, so they'll stop harassing you, so you can then do your own thing.

103
00:08:31,711 --> 00:08:36,772
[SPEAKER_01]: But when it comes to dhamma, that, like, as I said,

104
00:08:38,582 --> 00:08:45,787
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no room for compromise there, because people are already entirely leaning away from dhamma, with their lifestyle, with their desires, with everything else.

105
00:08:46,447 --> 00:09:00,156
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, if you would not want to say what the dhamma is, because the person who asked you a question might be upset, well, you're actually... that's quite a demeritorious act, to not say what the dhamma is, even if they get upset.

106
00:09:01,559 --> 00:09:02,859
[SPEAKER_00]: There was a sutra on that, isn't it?

107
00:09:03,119 --> 00:09:05,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it upsets him, well... Yeah, yeah.

108
00:09:05,521 --> 00:09:13,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, there is a difference between just going around and preaching and trying to convert everybody to your views and stuff, then people have a right to be upset.

109
00:09:13,605 --> 00:09:17,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if the Buddha himself is just teaching it, the purest dhamma.

110
00:09:18,248 --> 00:09:26,032
[SPEAKER_01]: But if a person comes to ask, comes to ask the question and wants to hear what you have to say, and then they get upset on account of what you have to say,

111
00:09:28,218 --> 00:09:31,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, basically, not... and, again, they will always... they will always try and...

112
00:09:32,481 --> 00:09:36,443
[SPEAKER_01]: I heard it many times, they will always try and say, oh, it's not what you said, it's how you said it.

113
00:09:37,304 --> 00:09:41,687
[SPEAKER_01]: And that just shows that, basically, it's not... because I could say it's not what you heard, it's how you heard it.

114
00:09:42,628 --> 00:09:43,388
[SPEAKER_01]: You could say the same.

115
00:09:43,928 --> 00:09:45,930
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just blaming the other person now for it.

116
00:09:46,730 --> 00:09:51,634
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is, if you're honest with yourself, you would... you would recognize, hold on, yes, I'm upset.

117
00:09:55,355 --> 00:10:02,178
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a pressure, there is a desire to blame it on how this person said it and how they come across.

118
00:10:03,519 --> 00:10:05,680
[SPEAKER_01]: But is that really why am I upset?

119
00:10:06,801 --> 00:10:12,423
[SPEAKER_01]: What if they said something that I agree with and they said in the same manner?

120
00:10:12,903 --> 00:10:13,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I be upset?

121
00:10:15,344 --> 00:10:16,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Most likely not.

122
00:10:17,005 --> 00:10:20,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the real reason I'm upset is because I don't like what I heard.

123
00:10:22,083 --> 00:10:25,665
[SPEAKER_01]: The message across, the meaning that I obviously got, enough to upset me.

124
00:10:26,005 --> 00:10:26,466
[SPEAKER_01]: I hate it.

125
00:10:27,526 --> 00:10:29,087
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to accept it, exactly.

126
00:10:29,227 --> 00:10:31,749
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's like, so then all these other reflections will come in place.

127
00:10:32,409 --> 00:10:33,070
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why?

128
00:10:33,850 --> 00:10:36,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I affected by hearing this?

129
00:10:37,032 --> 00:10:37,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Why I don't like it?

130
00:10:37,853 --> 00:10:39,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Why I resist it?

131
00:10:40,054 --> 00:10:41,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I irritated by it?

132
00:10:43,656 --> 00:10:46,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it goes against my desires or where my views are.

133
00:10:46,338 --> 00:10:49,620
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, well, that in and of itself, it's against the practice of the dhamma.

134
00:10:51,207 --> 00:10:57,368
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, like that sutta, where that somebody was criticizing the nuns, rightly or wrongly, doesn't matter.

135
00:10:57,588 --> 00:10:58,828
[SPEAKER_01]: I think he was wrongly, actually.

136
00:10:59,168 --> 00:11:01,349
[SPEAKER_01]: He was just criticizing the nuns for wrong reasons.

137
00:11:01,909 --> 00:11:05,470
[SPEAKER_01]: And this monk, there was... there was their friend, he got upset.

138
00:11:06,150 --> 00:11:08,850
[SPEAKER_01]: And the monk was told off by the Buddha.

139
00:11:09,850 --> 00:11:10,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he's becoming too intimate.

140
00:11:11,031 --> 00:11:12,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he became too intimate.

141
00:11:12,211 --> 00:11:16,131
[SPEAKER_01]: He got upset when somebody else was criticized, regardless of whether it was right or wrong criticism.

142
00:11:17,252 --> 00:11:18,792
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's the... Am I getting upset?

143
00:11:20,394 --> 00:11:21,342
[SPEAKER_00]: And you shouldn't get upset.

144
00:11:21,362 --> 00:11:22,008
[SPEAKER_00]: You shouldn't get upset.

145
00:11:22,028 --> 00:11:22,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if they get...

146
00:11:23,750 --> 00:11:24,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Tortured or whatever.

147
00:11:25,151 --> 00:11:26,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you shouldn't get upset even if you get tortured.

148
00:11:27,052 --> 00:11:27,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

149
00:11:27,793 --> 00:11:28,854
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone if it's not you.

150
00:11:29,954 --> 00:11:35,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean like you you ignore it or you don't know what's happening or as I said, you don't know whether the criticism is right or wrong.

151
00:11:35,878 --> 00:11:37,019
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's all secondary.

152
00:11:37,239 --> 00:11:38,860
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like, am I moved by this?

153
00:11:39,020 --> 00:11:41,842
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, means I'm not abandoning desire in regard to my own views.

154
00:11:42,983 --> 00:11:47,506
[SPEAKER_01]: And by doing that, by refusing to abandon it, I'm not practicing the dhamma even if I'm claiming to practice it.

155
00:11:47,846 --> 00:11:51,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you are really looking like you're supporting the dhamma.

156
00:11:51,269 --> 00:11:51,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

157
00:11:51,569 --> 00:11:51,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So

158
00:11:53,738 --> 00:11:58,883
[SPEAKER_00]: I support nuns, and anyone who's against nuns, that's wrong, and I will fight against them.

159
00:11:58,923 --> 00:11:59,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you irritated?

160
00:11:59,824 --> 00:12:00,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

161
00:12:00,405 --> 00:12:00,945
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why?

162
00:12:00,986 --> 00:12:01,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

163
00:12:01,386 --> 00:12:05,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Because within, you're maintaining hatred, lust.

164
00:12:05,390 --> 00:12:06,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not supporting the dhamma at all.

165
00:12:07,472 --> 00:12:07,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

166
00:12:08,353 --> 00:12:12,858
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all this outward work that you're doing is actually not going to benefit.

167
00:12:15,066 --> 00:12:19,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's not, exactly, it's not gonna, it's just gonna revolve around worldly things.

168
00:12:19,288 --> 00:12:24,371
[SPEAKER_01]: It's gonna become a worldly thing, even if that was not necessarily your intentional plan.

169
00:12:24,671 --> 00:12:34,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so, I mean, yeah, people want, you know, you want to help, you want to not be hurtful to others, you want to be cool, and so on.

170
00:12:34,155 --> 00:12:37,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you don't have to take a course in non-violent communication.

171
00:12:38,578 --> 00:12:39,518
[SPEAKER_00]: You just have to not hate them.

172
00:12:39,558 --> 00:12:39,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

173
00:12:40,518 --> 00:12:41,859
[SPEAKER_01]: And not be irritated by them.

174
00:12:41,899 --> 00:12:43,560
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a thing that was gonna hurt people.

175
00:12:44,619 --> 00:12:55,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Restrain your desires, relinquish your acquisitions, and there will be no basis for you being upset.

176
00:12:55,808 --> 00:13:00,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the basis for you being upset will shrink, depending on how much you give up these things.

177
00:13:01,812 --> 00:13:03,173
[SPEAKER_01]: It's as straightforward as that.

178
00:13:06,116 --> 00:13:09,618
[SPEAKER_00]: I think this reminded me of... there's...

179
00:13:12,028 --> 00:13:15,549
[SPEAKER_00]: There's these two words, ascetic and aesthetic.

180
00:13:16,349 --> 00:13:16,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Aesthetics.

181
00:13:17,269 --> 00:13:17,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

182
00:13:17,910 --> 00:13:18,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Aesthetics.

183
00:13:18,350 --> 00:13:18,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

184
00:13:18,810 --> 00:13:19,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Aesthetics and aesthetics.

185
00:13:19,630 --> 00:13:25,211
[SPEAKER_00]: I think that's the difference between... there's the ascetics, the ones who give up lust, hatred and delusion.

186
00:13:25,652 --> 00:13:26,632
[SPEAKER_01]: On the level that matters.

187
00:13:26,652 --> 00:13:26,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

188
00:13:26,832 --> 00:13:28,232
[SPEAKER_01]: And then there's the aesthetics.

189
00:13:28,452 --> 00:13:29,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Aesthetics.

190
00:13:30,133 --> 00:13:31,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Who just look like they've been given up.

191
00:13:31,873 --> 00:13:32,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

192
00:13:32,333 --> 00:13:35,514
[SPEAKER_01]: They're giving it up on the level that it doesn't really matter.

193
00:13:35,534 --> 00:13:35,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

194
00:13:36,214 --> 00:13:36,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

195
00:13:36,354 --> 00:13:38,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that would kind of be all the

196
00:13:39,818 --> 00:13:53,430
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, the extreme self-mortification practices, they were completely misguided in terms of the mind and mental intention and the reasons behind, which Buddha said they're as unbeneficial as sensual pleasures are, engaging with sensual pleasure is.

197
00:13:54,251 --> 00:13:58,475
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but, yeah, you could realize, you could see that, sure, you don't even have to go to these extremes.

198
00:13:59,349 --> 00:14:23,793
[SPEAKER_01]: To fall into that category, all you need to do is, again, are you are you reviewing or are you prioritizing, rather, are you prioritizing your conviction that offers you security and sense of pleasure and comfort or are you willing to to question it?

199
00:14:25,269 --> 00:14:32,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you willing to to to really reflect upon and allow doubt to arise, or allow yourself to hear things you don't want to hear?

200
00:14:33,074 --> 00:14:35,476
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if you dislike them, okay.

201
00:14:35,916 --> 00:14:37,137
[SPEAKER_00]: And, or you say, this is wrong.

202
00:14:38,038 --> 00:14:41,200
[SPEAKER_01]: If your mindset is completely wrong, it's like, okay, it might be wrong.

203
00:14:41,980 --> 00:14:50,206
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, I need to understand why this, which I just heard, that upsets me, it goes against what I think practice is, that goes against what I want practice to be.

204
00:14:51,507 --> 00:14:52,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it wrong?

205
00:14:52,903 --> 00:15:05,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it just because it goes against my desires or because I factually understand the contradiction in it and... And then, even if you understand the contradiction in it, are you relieved that you found the contradiction in it?

206
00:15:06,026 --> 00:15:07,947
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, it means you're still invested with your desires.

207
00:15:09,207 --> 00:15:11,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Why were you threatened in the first place?

208
00:15:12,288 --> 00:15:19,410
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were so sure where the practice is, you would not have been threatened by something that was presented as opposed to it.

209
00:15:22,167 --> 00:15:32,332
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, by not engaging in such reflections or by not allowing that to arise, you are on the level of self-mortification that just sticks to their conviction and then dismisses everything else.

210
00:15:34,953 --> 00:15:35,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Denial.

211
00:15:35,713 --> 00:15:35,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

212
00:15:38,054 --> 00:15:50,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's, again, it boils down to being free from those desires, from any desire, from any need of finding safety in the world, in the ownership, in possessions,

213
00:15:53,320 --> 00:16:02,797
[SPEAKER_01]: And if a person is genuinely practicing dhamma for those reasons, they won't be deterred by the manner of speaking or the presentation.

214
00:16:03,752 --> 00:16:06,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they are looking at the bigger picture, which is...

215
00:16:06,234 --> 00:16:07,054
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm about to die.

216
00:16:07,354 --> 00:16:10,376
[SPEAKER_01]: I might not have much time left to practice this rightly.

217
00:16:11,197 --> 00:16:17,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So, please don't waste my time with poetry and sugarcoating your expression.

218
00:16:17,161 --> 00:16:18,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Just tell me what I need to do.

219
00:16:19,182 --> 00:16:20,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Freedom from suffering.

220
00:16:20,223 --> 00:16:21,023
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I get there?

221
00:16:21,844 --> 00:16:23,325
[SPEAKER_00]: How does that come about?

222
00:16:23,785 --> 00:16:25,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Straight... the straight path.

223
00:16:25,787 --> 00:16:25,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

224
00:16:33,933 --> 00:16:36,996
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, of course, as I said, you use common sense either way.

225
00:16:38,277 --> 00:16:45,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not to say that now you just have to tell the hard, bitter truth to everyone in the face, even if they don't ask you.

226
00:16:46,064 --> 00:16:46,985
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just dumb.

227
00:16:48,406 --> 00:16:57,274
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you know the person, if they're here to learn from you and study, and you're mincing words, you are doing them a disservice.

228
00:17:04,985 --> 00:17:06,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's not... that's not compassionate.

229
00:17:06,946 --> 00:17:07,806
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

230
00:17:09,146 --> 00:17:18,009
[SPEAKER_01]: That's more like selfish for you, because you don't wanna come across... or basically, you don't wanna be disliked.

231
00:17:18,289 --> 00:17:19,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's put it that way.

232
00:17:19,670 --> 00:17:24,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, if I come across like this, this person might dislike me, other person might dislike me.

233
00:17:24,711 --> 00:17:29,293
[SPEAKER_01]: That's more important to me than actually telling them what I already know that can benefit them.

234
00:17:31,058 --> 00:17:35,240
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you don't really know the dhamma, I suppose.

235
00:17:36,181 --> 00:17:41,764
[SPEAKER_00]: You might, but you're obviously, maybe, not as established in it.

236
00:17:41,904 --> 00:17:49,408
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to prioritize that over the dhamma, means you need some development to do, because it hasn't settled in.

237
00:17:49,488 --> 00:17:53,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, if you're trying to cater your external... Cater, yeah.

238
00:17:54,211 --> 00:17:57,373
[SPEAKER_01]: For those reasons, means your conceit still overshadows

239
00:17:59,861 --> 00:18:01,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Any understanding that you might have.

240
00:18:02,343 --> 00:18:09,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Because uprooting lust, hatred, that's... that... that will sort... that... things will work out, you know.

241
00:18:09,348 --> 00:18:10,790
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just... that's the emphasis.

242
00:18:11,010 --> 00:18:11,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

243
00:18:11,690 --> 00:18:15,253
[SPEAKER_01]: But the key is, like, again, when you're asked.

244
00:18:16,134 --> 00:18:22,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Because sometimes you can equally put people off by telling them what they don't want to hear and they didn't ask you to hear it.

245
00:18:22,999 --> 00:18:23,179
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

246
00:18:23,800 --> 00:18:26,342
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's equally... that can be equally misguided.

247
00:18:26,602 --> 00:18:26,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

248
00:18:26,782 --> 00:18:27,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Because sometimes people get

249
00:18:28,667 --> 00:18:38,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Some understanding or some sort of conviction, and then they can't resist the urge to go and preach to everybody, you know, stand on the town square and shout to everybody, because this is the truth.

250
00:18:38,876 --> 00:18:39,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody asked you.

251
00:18:42,719 --> 00:18:52,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the Buddha himself, who actually, I mean, knew the clearest in all of its facets truth, had to be asked to teach.

252
00:18:53,247 --> 00:18:54,969
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't just...

253
00:18:55,409 --> 00:18:56,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Even to the monks.

254
00:18:57,112 --> 00:19:04,209
[SPEAKER_01]: On a number of occasions, Ānanda would recognize, that was the Ānanda's skill, to recognize the right time to ask the Lord for the teaching, and then the Buddha would teach him.

